# Predictions Screen: CNN Anomaly Detection for Laparoscopy Snapshots

A step-by-step guide to adding a **Predictions** screen to your desktop application, where a doctor uploads a snapshot and a CNN highlights and labels possible abnormalities.

> **Important:** This is a decision-support feature. It must be presented as "suggested findings for clinician review", never as a diagnosis. If the software will be used on real patients, it becomes medical device software (regulatory approval such as CE/FDA/local authority, clinical validation, and data-protection compliance are required). For a student/prototype project, label it clearly as a research prototype.

---

## 1. Decide what "detect anomalies" means (do this first)

The model type depends on what output you want. Pick one level and build up.

| Level | Task | Output | Difficulty | Data needed |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Image classification** | "Image shows: normal / endometriosis / inflammation..." + confidence | Easy | Image-level labels |
| 2 | **Classification + heatmap (Grad-CAM)** | Same as above + a heatmap showing *where* the model looked | Easy-Medium | Same as level 1 (no extra labels) |
| 3 | **Object detection (YOLO)** | Bounding boxes around each anomaly + label | Medium | Bounding box annotations |
| 4 | **Segmentation (U-Net)** | Pixel-level mask of the anomaly | Hard | Pixel-level masks |

**Recommendation:** Start with **Level 2** (classifier + Grad-CAM). It satisfies "point out the anomaly" without expensive annotation. Move to Level 3 only if you get box-annotated data.

You also need to decide **which anomaly/procedure** you target (e.g., gynecologic laparoscopy findings, cholecystectomy findings). A model can't be "generally abnormal-aware"; it needs a defined label set. Write your class list down before anything else, e.g.:

```
normal
endometriosis_lesion
adhesion
inflammation
other_abnormal
```

---

## 2. Build vs. use a pre-trained model: the answer

**Do not train a CNN from scratch.** Use **transfer learning**: take a CNN pre-trained on ImageNet and fine-tune it on your laparoscopy images.

| Option | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Train from scratch | Needs 100k+ images and lots of compute. Bad idea. |
| Use a ready-made model as-is | No good general "laparoscopy anomaly" model exists that you can trust for your label set. |
| **Fine-tune a pre-trained CNN** | **Best.** Works with a few thousand images, trains on a free GPU (Google Colab / Kaggle). |

Good backbones to fine-tune:

- **ResNet-50**: reliable baseline, easy Grad-CAM.
- **EfficientNet-B0 / B2**: better accuracy for size.
- **ConvNeXt-Tiny**: modern, strong.
- **MobileNetV3**: if the desktop app must run on weak hardware without GPU.

Start with **EfficientNet-B0 or ResNet-50**.

---

## 3. Get free laparoscopy images

Always check each dataset's license and access terms. Some need a registration or data-use agreement, and licenses can change. Most are **research/non-commercial only**.

| Dataset | Content | Good for |
|---|---|---|
| **GLENDA** (Gynecologic Laparoscopy ENdometriosis DAtaset) | Gynecologic laparoscopy images with endometriosis lesion annotations, plus normal images | **Best fit for real "abnormality" labels** |
| **LapGyn4** | 4 subsets of gynecologic laparoscopy images (anatomy, actions, surgical actions, instrument counts) | Extra normal/context images, pretraining |
| **Cholec80** | 80 cholecystectomy videos with tool and phase labels | Large volume of frames, pretraining |
| **CholecSeg8k** | Frames from Cholec80 with pixel masks of organs/tools | Segmentation practice |
| **Endoscapes / Endoscapes2023** | Cholecystectomy frames with segmentation and safety-view assessment | Segmentation/detection |
| **HeiChole** | Cholecystectomy video with phases, tools, actions | Frames for pretraining |
| **m2cai16-tool / m2cai16-workflow** | Cholecystectomy tool and phase data | Tool presence, extra frames |
| **SurgicalActions160** | Gynecologic laparoscopy action clips | Action recognition |
| **EndoVis challenges (MICCAI)** | Instrument segmentation, scene understanding | Segmentation |
| **Kvasir / Kvasir-SEG** | GI endoscopy (not laparoscopy) with polyps and lesion masks | Practicing the abnormality-detection pipeline, since it has real lesion labels |

Where to look:
- Dataset home pages (search the dataset name; e.g., GLENDA and LapGyn4 are hosted by ITEC, University of Klagenfurt).
- **Kaggle**, **Zenodo**, **Synapse**, **Papers with Code → Datasets**, **Hugging Face Datasets**.
- Search phrases: "laparoscopy dataset", "surgical video dataset", "endometriosis laparoscopy images".

**Reality check:** Most public laparoscopy datasets label *tools, phases, and anatomy*, not *pathology*. Real abnormality labels are scarcer (GLENDA is the main one). Plan for this:

1. Use GLENDA (or another labeled pathology set) for your actual classes.
2. Use the big datasets (Cholec80, LapGyn4) for **normal** samples or for a first-stage pretraining.
3. Later, add **your own app's snapshots**, labeled by a doctor. This is what makes the model good for *your* camera and lighting.

**Data privacy:** If you use snapshots from your own application, they are patient data. Get ethics approval and consent, strip all metadata (EXIF/DICOM tags), and crop out any on-screen patient info/overlays.

