## 4. Overall architecture

```
┌─────────────────────────┐        ┌────────────────────────────┐
│  Desktop App            │        │  Inference Service         │
│  ┌───────────────────┐  │  HTTP  │  (FastAPI + ONNX Runtime)  │
│  │ Predictions screen│──┼───────►│  - preprocess              │
│  │ - Upload image    │  │  JSON  │  - CNN forward pass        │
│  │ - Run analysis    │◄─┼────────│  - Grad-CAM heatmap        │
│  │ - Overlay results │  │        │  - return labels + overlay │
│  └───────────────────┘  │        └────────────────────────────┘
└─────────────────────────┘
```

Two ways to integrate the model:

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| **A. Local inference service** (FastAPI running on the same machine or LAN) | Easiest; keep Python for ML; works with any desktop language; easy updates | Need to start/stop the service with the app |
| **B. ONNX model embedded directly in the desktop app** (ONNX Runtime has C#, C++, Java, JS bindings) | Single installer, no Python at runtime, works offline | Grad-CAM is harder to embed; more integration work |

**Recommendation:** Build with **Approach A first**. Export to ONNX anyway, so you can move to Approach B later. For a hospital setting, keep inference **on-premises/offline**; do not send patient images to a public cloud.

